#: src/advanced-features.md:1
msgid "Advanced features"
msgstr ""

#: src/advanced-features.md:3
msgid ""
"The features listed on this page fall outside the rest of the main "
"categories."
msgstr ""

#: src/advanced-features.md:5
msgid ""
"`#[cfg(doc)]`: Documenting platform-specific or feature-specific information"
msgstr ""

#: src/advanced-features.md:7
msgid ""
"For conditional compilation, Rustdoc treats your crate the same way the "
"compiler does. Only things from the host target are available (or from the "
"given `--target` if present), and everything else is \"filtered out\" from "
"the crate. This can cause problems if your crate is providing different "
"things on different targets and you want your documentation to reflect all "
"the available items you provide."
msgstr ""

#: src/advanced-features.md:13
msgid ""
"If you want to make sure an item is seen by Rustdoc regardless of what "
"platform it's targeting, you can apply `#[cfg(doc)]` to it. Rustdoc sets "
"this whenever it's building documentation, so anything that uses that flag "
"will make it into documentation it generates. To apply this to an item with "
"other `#[cfg]` filters on it, you can write something like "
"`#[cfg(any(windows, doc))]`. This will preserve the item either when built "
"normally on Windows, or when being documented anywhere."
msgstr ""

#: src/advanced-features.md:20
msgid "Please note that this `cfg` is not passed to doctests."
msgstr ""

#: src/advanced-features.md:22
msgid "Example:"
msgstr ""

#: src/advanced-features.md:25
msgid "/// Token struct that can only be used on Windows.\n"
msgstr ""

#: src/advanced-features.md:27
msgid "/// Token struct that can only be used on Unix.\n"
msgstr ""

#: src/advanced-features.md:33
msgid ""
"Here, the respective tokens can only be used by dependent crates on their "
"respective platforms, but they will both appear in documentation."
msgstr ""

#: src/advanced-features.md:36
msgid "Interactions between platform-specific docs"
msgstr ""

#: src/advanced-features.md:38
msgid ""
"Rustdoc does not have a magic way to compile documentation 'as-if' you'd run "
"it once for each platform (such a magic wand has been called the ['holy "
"grail of rustdoc'](https://github.com/rust-lang/rust/issues/1998)). Instead, "
"it sees _all_ of your code at once, the same way the Rust compiler would if "
"you passed it `--cfg doc`. The main difference is that rustdoc doesn't run "
"all the compiler passes, meaning that some invalid code won't emit an error."
msgstr ""

#: src/advanced-features.md:46
msgid "Add aliases for an item in documentation search"
msgstr ""

#: src/advanced-features.md:48
msgid ""
"This feature allows you to add alias(es) to an item when using the `rustdoc` "
"search through the `doc(alias)` attribute. Example:"
msgstr ""

#: src/advanced-features.md:52 src/advanced-features.md:66
msgid "\"x\""
msgstr ""

#: src/advanced-features.md:53 src/advanced-features.md:66
msgid "\"big\""
msgstr ""

#: src/advanced-features.md:57
msgid ""
"Then, when looking for it through the `rustdoc` search, if you enter \"x\" "
"or \"big\", search will show the `BigX` struct first."
msgstr ""

#: src/advanced-features.md:60
msgid ""
"There are some limitations on the doc alias names though: they cannot "
"contain quotes (`'`, `\"`) or most whitespace. ASCII space is allowed if it "
"does not start or end the alias."
msgstr ""

#: src/advanced-features.md:63
msgid "You can add multiple aliases at the same time by using a list:"
msgstr ""

#: src/advanced-features.md:70
msgid "Custom search engines"
msgstr ""

#: src/advanced-features.md:72
msgid ""
"If you find yourself often referencing online Rust docs you might enjoy "
"using a custom search engine. This allows you to use the navigation bar "
"directly to search a `rustdoc` website. Most browsers support this feature "
"by letting you define a URL template containing `%s` which will be "
"substituted for the search term. As an example, for the standard library you "
"could use this template:"
msgstr ""

#: src/advanced-features.md:78
msgid ""
"```text\n"
"https://doc.rust-lang.org/stable/std/?search=%s\n"
"```"
msgstr ""

#: src/advanced-features.md:82
msgid ""
"Note that this will take you to a results page listing all matches. If you "
"want to navigate to the first result right away (which is often the best "
"match) use the following instead:"
msgstr ""

#: src/advanced-features.md:85
msgid ""
"```text\n"
"https://doc.rust-lang.org/stable/std/?search=%s&go_to_first=true\n"
"```"
msgstr ""

#: src/advanced-features.md:89
msgid ""
"This URL adds the `go_to_first=true` query parameter which can be appended "
"to any `rustdoc` search URL to automatically go to the first result."
msgstr ""

#: src/advanced-features.md:92
msgid "`#[repr(...)]`: Documenting the representation of a type"
msgstr ""

#: src/advanced-features.md:94
msgid ""
"Generally, rustdoc only displays the representation of a given type if none "
"of its variants are `#[doc(hidden)]` and if all of its fields are public and "
"not `#[doc(hidden)]` since it's likely not meant to be considered part of "
"the public ABI otherwise."
msgstr ""

#: src/advanced-features.md:98
msgid ""
"Note that there's no way to overwrite that heuristic and force rustdoc to "
"show the representation regardless."
msgstr ""

#: src/advanced-features.md:101
msgid "`#[repr(transparent)]`"
msgstr ""

#: src/advanced-features.md:103
msgid ""
"You can read more about `#[repr(transparent)]` itself in the [Rust Reference]"
"(https://doc.rust-lang.org/reference/type-layout.html#the-transparent-"
"representation) and in the [Rustonomicon](https://doc.rust-lang.org/nomicon/"
"other-reprs.html#reprtransparent)."
msgstr ""

#: src/advanced-features.md:106
msgid ""
"Since this representation is only considered part of the public ABI if the "
"single field with non-trivial size or alignment is public and if the "
"documentation does not state otherwise, rustdoc helpfully displays the "
"attribute if and only if the non-1-ZST field is public and not "
"`#[doc(hidden)]` or – in case all fields are 1-ZST fields — at least one "
"field is public and not `#[doc(hidden)]`. The term _1-ZST_ refers to types "
"that are one-aligned and zero-sized."
msgstr ""

#: src/advanced-features.md:112
msgid ""
"It would seem that one can manually hide the attribute with "
"`#[cfg_attr(not(doc), repr(transparent))]` if one wishes to declare the "
"representation as private even if the non-1-ZST field is public. However, "
"due to [current limitations](https://github.com/rust-lang/rust/"
"issues/114952), this method is not always guaranteed to work. Therefore, if "
"you would like to do so, you should always write that down in prose "
"independently of whether you use `cfg_attr` or not."
msgstr ""

#: src/command-line-arguments.md:1
msgid "Command-line arguments"
msgstr ""

#: src/command-line-arguments.md:3
msgid "Here's the list of arguments you can pass to `rustdoc`:"
msgstr ""

#: src/command-line-arguments.md:5
msgid "`-h`/`--help`: help"
msgstr ""

#: src/command-line-arguments.md:7 src/command-line-arguments.md:22
#: src/command-line-arguments.md:38 src/command-line-arguments.md:62
#: src/command-line-arguments.md:76 src/command-line-arguments.md:88
#: src/command-line-arguments.md:108 src/command-line-arguments.md:125
#: src/command-line-arguments.md:140 src/command-line-arguments.md:151
#: src/command-line-arguments.md:163 src/command-line-arguments.md:188
#: src/command-line-arguments.md:201 src/command-line-arguments.md:214
#: src/command-line-arguments.md:249 src/command-line-arguments.md:262
#: src/command-line-arguments.md:282 src/command-line-arguments.md:301
#: src/command-line-arguments.md:313 src/command-line-arguments.md:326
#: src/command-line-arguments.md:340 src/command-line-arguments.md:352
#: src/command-line-arguments.md:365 src/command-line-arguments.md:377
#: src/command-line-arguments.md:388 src/command-line-arguments.md:401
#: src/command-line-arguments.md:415 src/command-line-arguments.md:433
msgid "Using this flag looks like this:"
msgstr ""

#: src/command-line-arguments.md:14
msgid ""
"This will show `rustdoc`'s built-in help, which largely consists of a list "
"of possible command-line flags."
msgstr ""

#: src/command-line-arguments.md:17
msgid ""
"Some of `rustdoc`'s flags are unstable; this page only shows stable options, "
"`--help` will show them all."
msgstr ""

#: src/command-line-arguments.md:20
msgid "`-V`/`--version`: version information"
msgstr ""

#: src/command-line-arguments.md:29
msgid ""
"This will show `rustdoc`'s version, which will look something like this:"
msgstr ""

#: src/command-line-arguments.md:36
msgid "`-v`/`--verbose`: more verbose output"
msgstr ""

#: src/command-line-arguments.md:45
msgid ""
"This enables \"verbose mode\", which means that more information will be "
"written to standard out. What is written depends on the other flags you've "
"passed in. For example, with `--version`:"
msgstr ""

#: src/command-line-arguments.md:60
msgid "`-o`/`--out-dir`: output directory path"
msgstr ""

#: src/command-line-arguments.md:69
msgid ""
"By default, `rustdoc`'s output appears in a directory named `doc` in the "
"current working directory. With this flag, it will place all output into the "
"directory you specify."
msgstr ""

#: src/command-line-arguments.md:74
msgid "`--crate-name`: controlling the name of the crate"
msgstr ""

#: src/command-line-arguments.md:82
msgid ""
"By default, `rustdoc` assumes that the name of your crate is the same name "
"as the `.rs` file. `--crate-name` lets you override this assumption with "
"whatever name you choose."
msgstr ""

#: src/command-line-arguments.md:86
msgid "`--document-private-items`: Show items that are not public"
msgstr ""

#: src/command-line-arguments.md:94
msgid "By default, `rustdoc` only documents items that are publicly reachable."
msgstr ""

#: src/command-line-arguments.md:97
msgid "// this item is public and will be documented\n"
msgstr ""

#: src/command-line-arguments.md:98
msgid "// this item is private and will not be documented\n"
msgstr ""

#: src/command-line-arguments.md:99
msgid "// this item is public, but unreachable, so it will not be documented\n"
msgstr ""

#: src/command-line-arguments.md:103
msgid ""
"`--document-private-items` includes all non-public items in the generated "
"documentation except for `#[doc(hidden)]` items.  Private items will be "
"shown with a 🔒 icon."
msgstr ""

#: src/command-line-arguments.md:106
msgid "`-L`/`--library-path`: where to look for dependencies"
msgstr ""

#: src/command-line-arguments.md:115
msgid ""
"If your crate has dependencies, `rustdoc` needs to know where to find them. "
"Passing `--library-path` gives `rustdoc` a list of places to look for these "
"dependencies."
msgstr ""

#: src/command-line-arguments.md:119
msgid ""
"This flag takes any number of directories as its argument, and will use all "
"of them when searching."
msgstr ""

#: src/command-line-arguments.md:123
msgid "`--cfg`: passing configuration flags"
msgstr ""

#: src/command-line-arguments.md:128
msgid "\"foo\""
msgstr ""

#: src/command-line-arguments.md:131
msgid ""
"This flag accepts the same values as `rustc --cfg`, and uses it to configure "
"compilation. The example above uses `feature`, but any of the `cfg` values "
"are acceptable."
msgstr ""

#: src/command-line-arguments.md:135
msgid "`--check-cfg`: check configuration flags"
msgstr ""

#: src/command-line-arguments.md:137
msgid ""
"This flag accepts the same values as `rustc --check-cfg`, and uses it to "
"check configuration flags."
msgstr ""

#: src/command-line-arguments.md:143
msgid "'cfg(my_cfg, values(\"foo\", \"bar\"))'"
msgstr ""

#: src/command-line-arguments.md:146
msgid ""
"The example above check every well known names and values (`target_os`, "
"`doc`, `test`, ...) and check the values of `my_cfg`: `foo` and `bar`."
msgstr ""

#: src/command-line-arguments.md:149
msgid "`--extern`: specify a dependency's location"
msgstr ""

#: src/command-line-arguments.md:157
msgid ""
"Similar to `--library-path`, `--extern` is about specifying the location of "
"a dependency. `--library-path` provides directories to search in, `--extern` "
"instead lets you specify exactly which dependency is located where."
msgstr ""

#: src/command-line-arguments.md:161
msgid "`-C`/`--codegen`: pass codegen options to rustc"
msgstr ""

#: src/command-line-arguments.md:176
msgid ""
"When rustdoc generates documentation, looks for documentation tests, or "
"executes documentation tests, it needs to compile some rust code, at least "
"part-way. This flag allows you to tell rustdoc to provide some extra codegen "
"options to rustc when it runs these compilations. Most of the time, these "
"options won't affect a regular documentation run, but if something depends "
"on target features to be enabled, or documentation tests need to use some "
"additional options, this flag allows you to affect that."
msgstr ""

#: src/command-line-arguments.md:183
msgid ""
"The arguments to this flag are the same as those for the `-C` flag on rustc. "
"Run `rustc -C help` to get the full list."
msgstr ""

#: src/command-line-arguments.md:186
msgid "`--test`: run code examples as tests"
msgstr ""

#: src/command-line-arguments.md:194
msgid ""
"This flag will run your code examples as tests. For more, see [the chapter "
"on documentation tests](write-documentation/documentation-tests.md)."
msgstr ""

#: src/command-line-arguments.md:197
msgid "See also `--test-args` and `--test-run-directory`."
msgstr ""

#: src/command-line-arguments.md:199
msgid "`--test-args`: pass options to test runner"
msgstr ""

#: src/command-line-arguments.md:207
msgid ""
"This flag will pass options to the test runner when running documentation "
"tests. For more, see [the chapter on documentation tests](write-"
"documentation/documentation-tests.md)."
msgstr ""

#: src/command-line-arguments.md:210 src/command-line-arguments.md:223
msgid "See also `--test`."
msgstr ""

#: src/command-line-arguments.md:212
msgid "`--test-run-directory`: run code examples in a specific directory"
msgstr ""

#: src/command-line-arguments.md:220
msgid ""
"This flag will run your code examples in the specified working directory. "
"For more, see [the chapter on documentation tests](write-documentation/"
"documentation-tests.md)."
msgstr ""

#: src/command-line-arguments.md:225
msgid ""
"`--test-runtool`, `--test-runtool-arg`: program to run tests with; args to "
"pass to it"
msgstr ""

#: src/command-line-arguments.md:227
msgid ""
"A doctest wrapper program can be specified with the `--test-runtool` flag. "
"Rustdoc will execute that wrapper instead of the doctest executable when "
"running tests. The first arguments to the wrapper will be any arguments "
"specified with the `--test-runtool-arg` flag, followed by the path to the "
"doctest executable to run."
msgstr ""

#: src/command-line-arguments.md:233
msgid "Using these options looks like this:"
msgstr ""

#: src/command-line-arguments.md:239
msgid ""
"For example, if you want to run your doctests under valgrind you might run:"
msgstr ""

#: src/command-line-arguments.md:245
msgid ""
"Another use case would be to run a test inside an emulator, or through a "
"Virtual Machine."
msgstr ""

#: src/command-line-arguments.md:247
msgid "`--target`: generate documentation for the specified target triple"
msgstr ""

#: src/command-line-arguments.md:255
msgid ""
"Similar to the `--target` flag for `rustc`, this generates documentation for "
"a target triple that's different than your host triple."
msgstr ""

#: src/command-line-arguments.md:258
msgid "All of the usual caveats of cross-compiling code apply."
msgstr ""

#: src/command-line-arguments.md:260
msgid "`--default-theme`: set the default theme"
msgstr ""

#: src/command-line-arguments.md:268
msgid ""
"Sets the default theme (for users whose browser has not remembered a "
"previous theme selection from the on-page theme picker)."
msgstr ""

#: src/command-line-arguments.md:271
msgid ""
"The supplied value should be the lowercase version of the theme name. The "
"set of available themes can be seen in the theme picker in the generated "
"output."
msgstr ""

#: src/command-line-arguments.md:275
msgid ""
"Note that the set of available themes - and their appearance - is not "
"necessarily stable from one rustdoc version to the next.  If the requested "
"theme does not exist, the builtin default (currently `light`) is used "
"instead."
msgstr ""

#: src/command-line-arguments.md:280
msgid "`--markdown-css`: include more CSS files when rendering markdown"
msgstr ""

#: src/command-line-arguments.md:288
msgid ""
"When rendering Markdown files, this will create a `<link>` element in the "
"`<head>` section of the generated HTML. For example, with the invocation "
"above,"
msgstr ""

#: src/command-line-arguments.md:292
msgid "\"stylesheet\""
msgstr ""

#: src/command-line-arguments.md:292
msgid "\"text/css\""
msgstr ""

#: src/command-line-arguments.md:292
msgid "\"foo.css\""
msgstr ""

#: src/command-line-arguments.md:295
msgid "will be added."
msgstr ""

#: src/command-line-arguments.md:297
msgid "When rendering Rust files, this flag is ignored."
msgstr ""

#: src/command-line-arguments.md:299
msgid "`--html-in-header`: include more HTML in `<head>`"
msgstr ""

#: src/command-line-arguments.md:308
msgid ""
"This flag takes a list of files, and inserts them into the `<head>` section "
"of the rendered documentation."
msgstr ""

#: src/command-line-arguments.md:311
msgid "`--html-before-content`: include more HTML before the content"
msgstr ""

#: src/command-line-arguments.md:320
msgid ""
"This flag takes a list of files, and inserts them inside the `<body>` tag "
"but before the other content `rustdoc` would normally produce in the "
"rendered documentation."
msgstr ""

#: src/command-line-arguments.md:324
msgid "`--html-after-content`: include more HTML after the content"
msgstr ""

#: src/command-line-arguments.md:333
msgid ""
"This flag takes a list of files, and inserts them before the `</body>` tag "
"but after the other content `rustdoc` would normally produce in the rendered "
"documentation."
msgstr ""

#: src/command-line-arguments.md:338
msgid "`--markdown-playground-url`: control the location of the playground"
msgstr ""

#: src/command-line-arguments.md:346
msgid ""
"When rendering a Markdown file, this flag gives the base URL of the Rust "
"Playground, to use for generating `Run` buttons."
msgstr ""

#: src/command-line-arguments.md:350
msgid "`--markdown-no-toc`: don't generate a table of contents"
msgstr ""

#: src/command-line-arguments.md:358
msgid ""
"When generating documentation from a Markdown file, by default, `rustdoc` "
"will generate a table of contents. This flag suppresses that, and no TOC "
"will be generated."
msgstr ""

#: src/command-line-arguments.md:363
msgid "`-e`/`--extend-css`: extend rustdoc's CSS"
msgstr ""

#: src/command-line-arguments.md:372
msgid ""
"With this flag, the contents of the files you pass are included at the "
"bottom of the `theme.css` file."
msgstr ""

#: src/command-line-arguments.md:375
msgid "`--sysroot`: override the system root"
msgstr ""

#: src/command-line-arguments.md:383
msgid ""
"Similar to `rustc --sysroot`, this lets you change the sysroot `rustdoc` "
"uses when compiling your code."
msgstr ""

#: src/command-line-arguments.md:386
msgid "`--edition`: control the edition of docs and doctests"
msgstr ""

#: src/command-line-arguments.md:395
msgid ""
"This flag allows `rustdoc` to treat your rust code as the given edition. It "
"will compile doctests with the given edition as well. As with `rustc`, the "
"default edition that `rustdoc` will use is `2015` (the first edition)."
msgstr ""

#: src/command-line-arguments.md:399
msgid "`--theme`: add a theme to the documentation output"
msgstr ""

#: src/command-line-arguments.md:407
msgid ""
"`rustdoc`'s default output includes two themes: `light` (the default) and "
"`dark`. This flag allows you to add custom themes to the output. Giving a "
"CSS file to this flag adds it to your documentation as an additional theme "
"choice. The theme's name is determined by its filename; a theme file named "
"`custom-theme.css` will add a theme named `custom-theme` to the "
"documentation."
msgstr ""

#: src/command-line-arguments.md:413
msgid "`--check-theme`: verify custom themes against the default theme"
msgstr ""

#: src/command-line-arguments.md:421
msgid ""
"While `rustdoc`'s HTML output is more-or-less consistent between versions, "
"there is no guarantee that a theme file will have the same effect. The `--"
"theme` flag will still allow you to add the theme to your documentation, but "
"to ensure that your theme works as expected, you can use this flag to verify "
"that it implements the same CSS rules as the official `light` theme."
msgstr ""

#: src/command-line-arguments.md:427
msgid ""
"`--check-theme` is a separate mode in `rustdoc`. When `rustdoc` sees the `--"
"check-theme` flag, it discards all other flags and only performs the CSS "
"rule comparison operation."
msgstr ""

#: src/command-line-arguments.md:431
msgid "`--crate-version`: control the crate version"
msgstr ""

#: src/command-line-arguments.md:439
msgid ""
"When `rustdoc` receives this flag, it will print an extra \"Version "
"(version)\" into the sidebar of the crate root's docs. You can use this flag "
"to differentiate between different versions of your library's documentation."
msgstr ""

#: src/command-line-arguments.md:443
msgid "`-`: load source code from the standard input"
msgstr ""

#: src/command-line-arguments.md:445
msgid ""
"If you specify `-` as the INPUT on the command line, then `rustdoc` will "
"read the source code from stdin (standard input stream) until the EOF, "
"instead of the file system with an otherwise specified path."
msgstr ""

#: src/command-line-arguments.md:449
msgid "`@path`: load command-line flags from a path"
msgstr ""

#: src/command-line-arguments.md:451
msgid ""
"If you specify `@path` on the command-line, then it will open `path` and "
"read command line options from it. These options are one per line; a blank "
"line indicates an empty option. The file can use Unix or Windows style line "
"endings, and must be encoded as UTF-8."
msgstr ""

#: src/command-line-arguments.md:456
msgid "`--passes`: add more rustdoc passes"
msgstr ""

#: src/command-line-arguments.md:458 src/command-line-arguments.md:463
msgid ""
"This flag is **deprecated**. For more details on passes, see [the chapter on "
"them](deprecated-features.md#passes)."
msgstr ""

#: src/command-line-arguments.md:461
msgid "`--no-defaults`: don't run default passes"
msgstr ""

#: src/command-line-arguments.md:466
msgid "`-r`/`--input-format`: input format"
msgstr ""

#: src/command-line-arguments.md:468
msgid "This flag is **deprecated** and **has no effect**."
msgstr ""

#: src/command-line-arguments.md:470
msgid ""
"Rustdoc only supports Rust source code and Markdown input formats. If the "
"file ends in `.md` or `.markdown`, `rustdoc` treats it as a Markdown file. "
"Otherwise, it assumes that the input file is Rust."
msgstr ""

#: src/deprecated-features.md:1
msgid "Deprecated features"
msgstr ""

#: src/deprecated-features.md:3
msgid "Passes"
msgstr ""

#: src/deprecated-features.md:5
msgid ""
"Rustdoc has a concept called \"passes\". These are transformations that "
"`rustdoc` runs on your documentation before producing its final output."
msgstr ""

#: src/deprecated-features.md:8
msgid ""
"Customizing passes is **deprecated**. The available passes are not "
"considered stable and may change in any release."
msgstr ""

#: src/deprecated-features.md:11
msgid ""
"In the past the most common use case for customizing passes was to omit the "
"`strip-private` pass. You can do this more easily, and without risk of the "
"pass being changed, by passing [`--document-private-items`](command-line-"
"arguments.md#--document-private-items-show-items-that-are-not-public)."
msgstr ""

#: src/how-to-read-rustdoc.md:1
msgid "How to read rustdoc output"
msgstr ""

#: src/how-to-read-rustdoc.md:3
msgid ""
"Rustdoc's HTML output includes a friendly and useful navigation interface "
"which makes it easier for users to navigate and understand your code. This "
"chapter covers the major features of that interface, and is a great starting "
"point for documentation authors and users alike."
msgstr ""

#: src/how-to-read-rustdoc.md:8
msgid "Structure"
msgstr ""

#: src/how-to-read-rustdoc.md:10
msgid ""
"The `rustdoc` output is divided into three sections. Along the left side of "
"each page is a quick navigation bar, which shows contextual information "
"about the current entry. The rest of the page is taken up by the search "
"interface at the top and the documentation for the current item below that."
msgstr ""

#: src/how-to-read-rustdoc.md:16
msgid "The Item Documentation"
msgstr ""

#: src/how-to-read-rustdoc.md:18
msgid ""
"The majority of the screen is taken up with the documentation text for the "
"item currently being viewed. At the top is some at-a-glance info and "
"controls:"
msgstr ""

#: src/how-to-read-rustdoc.md:22
msgid ""
"the type and name of the item, such as \"Struct `std::time::Duration`\","
msgstr ""

#: src/how-to-read-rustdoc.md:24
msgid ""
"a button to copy the item's path to the clipboard, which is a clipboard item"
msgstr ""

#: src/how-to-read-rustdoc.md:26
msgid ""
"a button to collapse or expand the top-level documentation for that item "
"(`[+]` or `[-]`),"
msgstr ""

#: src/how-to-read-rustdoc.md:28
msgid ""
"a link to the source code (`[src]`), if [configured](write-documentation/the-"
"doc-attribute.html#html_no_source), and present (the source may not be "
"available if the documentation was created with `cargo doc --no-deps`),"
msgstr ""

#: src/how-to-read-rustdoc.md:32
msgid ""
"and the version in which the item became stable, if it's a stable item in "
"the standard library."
msgstr ""

#: src/how-to-read-rustdoc.md:35
msgid ""
"Below this is the main documentation for the item, including a definition or "
"function signature if appropriate, followed by a list of fields or variants "
"for Rust types. Finally, the page lists associated functions and trait "
"implementations, including automatic and blanket implementations that "
"`rustdoc` knows about."
msgstr ""

#: src/how-to-read-rustdoc.md:41
msgid "Sections"
msgstr ""

#: src/how-to-read-rustdoc.md:48
msgid "Aliased Type"
msgstr ""

#: src/how-to-read-rustdoc.md:50
msgid ""
"A type alias is expanded at compile time to its [aliased type](https://doc."
"rust-lang.org/reference/items/type-aliases.html). That may involve "
"substituting some or all of the type parameters in the target type with "
"types provided by the type alias definition. The Aliased Type section shows "
"the result of this expansion, including the types of public fields or "
"variants, which may depend on those substitutions."
msgstr ""

#: src/how-to-read-rustdoc.md:57
msgid "Navigation"
msgstr ""

#: src/how-to-read-rustdoc.md:59
msgid ""
"Subheadings, variants, fields, and many other things in this documentation "
"are anchors and can be clicked on and deep-linked to, which is a great way "
"to communicate exactly what you're talking about. The typographical "
"character \"§\" appears next to lines with anchors on them when hovered or "
"given keyboard focus."
msgstr ""

#: src/how-to-read-rustdoc.md:65
msgid "The Navigation Bar"
msgstr ""

#: src/how-to-read-rustdoc.md:67
msgid ""
"For example, when looking at documentation for the crate root, it shows all "
"the crates documented in the documentation bundle, and quick links to the "
"modules, structs, traits, functions, and macros available from the current "
"crate. At the top, it displays a [configurable logo](write-documentation/the-"
"doc-attribute.html#html_logo_url) alongside the current crate's name and "
"version, or the current item whose documentation is being displayed."
msgstr ""

#: src/how-to-read-rustdoc.md:75
msgid "The Theme Picker and Search Interface"
msgstr ""

#: src/how-to-read-rustdoc.md:77
msgid ""
"When viewing `rustdoc`'s output in a browser with JavaScript enabled, a "
"dynamic interface appears at the top of the page composed of the [search]"
"(read-documentation/search.md) interface, help screen, and [options](read-"
"documentation/in-doc-settings.html)."
msgstr ""

#: src/how-to-read-rustdoc.md:84
msgid ""
"Paths are supported as well, you can look for `Vec::new` or `Option::Some` "
"or even `module::module_child::another_child::struct::field`. Whitespace "
"characters are considered the same as `::`, so if you write `Vec    new`, it "
"will be considered the same as `Vec::new`."
msgstr ""

#: src/how-to-read-rustdoc.md:89
msgid "Shortcuts"
msgstr ""

#: src/how-to-read-rustdoc.md:91
msgid ""
"Pressing `S` while focused elsewhere on the page will move focus to the "
"search bar, and pressing `?` shows the help screen, which includes all these "
"shortcuts and more."
msgstr ""

#: src/how-to-read-rustdoc.md:95
msgid ""
"When the search results are focused, the left and right arrows move between "
"tabs and the up and down arrows move among the results. Pressing the enter "
"or return key opens the highlighted result."
msgstr ""

#: src/how-to-read-rustdoc.md:100
msgid ""
"When looking at the documentation for an item, the plus and minus keys "
"expand and collapse all sections in the document."
msgstr ""

#: src/read-documentation/in-doc-settings.md:1
msgid "Rustdoc in-doc settings"
msgstr ""

#: src/read-documentation/in-doc-settings.md:3
msgid ""
"Rustdoc's HTML output includes a settings menu, and this chapter describes "
"what each setting in this menu does."
msgstr ""

#: src/read-documentation/in-doc-settings.md:6
msgid ""
"It can be accessed by clicking on the gear button (<i class=\"fas fa-gear\" "
"aria-hidden=\"true\"></i>) in the upper right."
msgstr ""

#: src/read-documentation/in-doc-settings.md:9
msgid "Changing displayed theme"
msgstr ""

#: src/read-documentation/in-doc-settings.md:11
msgid ""
"It is possible to change the theme. If you pick the \"system preference\", "
"you will be able to see two new sub-menus: \"Preferred light theme\" and "
"\"Preferred dark theme\". It means that if your system preference is set to "
"\"light\", then rustdoc will use the theme you selected in \"Preferred light "
"theme\"."
msgstr ""

#: src/read-documentation/in-doc-settings.md:16
msgid "Auto-hide item contents for large items"
msgstr ""

#: src/read-documentation/in-doc-settings.md:18
msgid ""
"If the type definition contains more than 12 items, and this setting is "
"enabled, it'll collapse them by default. You can see them by clicking on the "
"`[+]` button to expand them."
msgstr ""

#: src/read-documentation/in-doc-settings.md:22
msgid ""
"A good example of this setting in use can be seen in the [`Iterator`]"
"(https://doc.rust-lang.org/stable/std/iter/trait.Iterator.html) doc page:"
msgstr ""

#: src/read-documentation/in-doc-settings.md:26
msgid "![Collapsed long item](../images/collapsed-long-item.png)"
msgstr ""

#: src/read-documentation/in-doc-settings.md:28
msgid "Auto-hide item methods' documentation"
msgstr ""

#: src/read-documentation/in-doc-settings.md:30
msgid ""
"If enabled, this setting will collapse all trait implementations blocks. It "
"is convenient if you just want an overview of all the methods available. You "
"can still see a method's documentation by expanding it."
msgstr ""

#: src/read-documentation/in-doc-settings.md:34
msgid "Auto-hide trait implementation documentation"
msgstr ""

#: src/read-documentation/in-doc-settings.md:36
msgid ""
"If enabled, this setting will collapse all trait implementations blocks (you "
"can see them in the \"Trait Implementations\" section). It is convenient if "
"you just want an overview of all the trait implemented on a type. You can "
"still see a trait implementation's associated items by expanding it."
msgstr ""

#: src/read-documentation/in-doc-settings.md:43
msgid "![Collapsed trait implementations](../images/collapsed-trait-impls.png)"
msgstr ""

#: src/read-documentation/in-doc-settings.md:45
msgid "Directly go to item in search if there is only one result"
msgstr ""

#: src/read-documentation/in-doc-settings.md:47
msgid ""
"If this setting is enabled, you will directly be taken to the result page if "
"your search only returned one element. Useful if you know exactly what "
"you're looking for and want to be taken there directly and not waste time "
"selecting the only search result."
msgstr ""

#: src/read-documentation/in-doc-settings.md:52
msgid "Show line numbers on code examples"
msgstr ""

#: src/read-documentation/in-doc-settings.md:54
msgid ""
"If enabled, this setting will add line numbers to the code examples in the "
"documentation. It provides a visual aide for the code reading."
msgstr ""

#: src/read-documentation/in-doc-settings.md:57
msgid "Disable keyboard shortcuts"
msgstr ""

#: src/read-documentation/in-doc-settings.md:59
msgid ""
"If this setting is enabled, the keyboard shortcuts will be disabled. It's "
"useful in case some of these shortcuts are already used by a web extension "
"you're using."
msgstr ""

#: src/read-documentation/in-doc-settings.md:63
msgid ""
"To see the full list of the rustdoc keyboard shortcuts, you can open the "
"help menu (the button with the question mark on the left of the setting menu "
"button)."
msgstr ""

#: src/read-documentation/search.md:1
msgid "Rustdoc search"
msgstr ""

#: src/read-documentation/search.md:3
msgid ""
"Typing in the search bar instantly searches the available documentation, "
"matching either the name and path of an item, or a function's approximate "
"type signature."
msgstr ""

#: src/read-documentation/search.md:7
msgid "Search By Name"
msgstr ""

#: src/read-documentation/search.md:9
msgid ""
"To search by the name of an item (items include modules, types, traits, "
"functions, and macros), write its name or path. As a special case, the parts "
"of a path that normally get divided by `::` double colons can instead be "
"separated by spaces. For example:"
msgstr ""

#: src/read-documentation/search.md:14
msgid ""
"[`vec new`](../../std/vec/struct.Vec.html?search=vec%20new&filter-crate=std) "
"and [`vec::new`](../../std/vec/struct.Vec.html?search=vec::new&filter-"
"crate=std) both show the function `std::vec::Vec::new` as a result."
msgstr ""

#: src/read-documentation/search.md:16
msgid ""
"[`vec`](../../std/vec/struct.Vec.html?search=vec&filter-crate=std), [`vec "
"vec`](../../std/vec/struct.Vec.html?search=vec%20vec&filter-crate=std), "
"[`std::vec`](../../std/vec/struct.Vec.html?search=std::vec&filter-"
"crate=std), and [`std::vec::Vec`](../../std/vec/struct.Vec.html?search=std::"
"vec::Vec&filter-crate=std) all include the struct `std::vec::Vec` itself in "
"the results (and all but the last one also include the module in the "
"results)."
msgstr ""

#: src/read-documentation/search.md:28
msgid ""
"As a quick way to trim down the list of results, there's a drop-down "
"selector below the search input, labeled \"Results in \\[std\\]\". Clicking "
"it can change which crate is being searched."
msgstr ""

#: src/read-documentation/search.md:32
msgid ""
"Rustdoc uses a fuzzy matching function that can tolerate typos for this, "
"though it's based on the length of the name that's typed in, so a good "
"example of how this works would be [`HahsMap`](../../std/collections/struct."
"HashMap.html?search=HahsMap&filter-crate=std). To avoid this, wrap the item "
"in quotes, searching for `\"HahsMap\"` (in this example, no results will be "
"returned)."
msgstr ""

#: src/read-documentation/search.md:39
msgid "Tabs in the Search By Name interface"
msgstr ""

#: src/read-documentation/search.md:41
msgid ""
"In fact, using [`HahsMap`](../../std/collections/struct.HashMap.html?"
"search=HahsMap&filter-crate=std) again as the example, it tells you that "
"you're using \"In Names\" by default, but also lists two other tabs below "
"the crate drop-down: \"In Parameters\" and \"In Return Types\"."
msgstr ""

#: src/read-documentation/search.md:45
msgid ""
"These two tabs are lists of functions, defined on the closest matching type "
"to the search (for `HahsMap`, it loudly auto-corrects to `hashmap`). This "
"auto-correct only kicks in if nothing is found that matches the literal."
msgstr ""

#: src/read-documentation/search.md:49
msgid ""
"These tabs are not just methods. For example, searching the alloc crate for "
"[`Layout`](../../alloc/index.html?search=Layout&filter-crate=alloc) also "
"lists functions that accept layouts even though they're methods on the "
"allocator or free functions."
msgstr ""

#: src/read-documentation/search.md:55
msgid "Searching By Type Signature"
msgstr ""

#: src/read-documentation/search.md:57
msgid ""
"If you know more specifically what the function you want to look at does, or "
"you want to know how to get from one type to another, Rustdoc can search by "
"more than one type at once in the parameters and return value. Multiple "
"parameters are separated by `,` commas, and the return value is written with "
"after a `->` arrow."
msgstr ""

