#: src/write-documentation/documentation-tests.md:1
msgid "Documentation tests"
msgstr ""

#: src/write-documentation/documentation-tests.md:3
msgid ""
"`rustdoc` supports executing your documentation examples as tests. This "
"makes sure that examples within your documentation are up to date and "
"working."
msgstr ""

#: src/write-documentation/documentation-tests.md:6
msgid "The basic idea is this:"
msgstr ""

#: src/write-documentation/documentation-tests.md:9
msgid ""
"/// # Examples\n"
"///\n"
"/// ```\n"
"/// let x = 5;\n"
"/// ```\n"
msgstr ""

#: src/write-documentation/documentation-tests.md:17
msgid ""
"The triple backticks start and end code blocks. If this were in a file named "
"`foo.rs`, running `rustdoc --test foo.rs` will extract this example, and "
"then run it as a test."
msgstr ""

#: src/write-documentation/documentation-tests.md:20
msgid ""
"Please note that by default, if no language is set for the block code, "
"rustdoc assumes it is Rust code. So the following:"
msgstr ""

#: src/write-documentation/documentation-tests.md:29
msgid "is strictly equivalent to:"
msgstr ""

#: src/write-documentation/documentation-tests.md:37
msgid "There's some subtlety though! Read on for more details."
msgstr ""

#: src/write-documentation/documentation-tests.md:39
msgid "Passing or failing a doctest"
msgstr ""

#: src/write-documentation/documentation-tests.md:41
msgid ""
"Like regular unit tests, regular doctests are considered to \"pass\" if they "
"compile and run without panicking. So if you want to demonstrate that some "
"computation gives a certain result, the `assert!` family of macros works the "
"same as other Rust code:"
msgstr ""

#: src/write-documentation/documentation-tests.md:51
msgid ""
"This way, if the computation ever returns something different, the code "
"panics and the doctest fails."
msgstr ""

#: src/write-documentation/documentation-tests.md:54
msgid "Pre-processing examples"
msgstr ""

#: src/write-documentation/documentation-tests.md:56
msgid ""
"In the example above, you'll note something strange: there's no `main` "
"function! Forcing you to write `main` for every example, no matter how "
"small, adds friction and clutters the output. So `rustdoc` processes your "
"examples slightly before running them. Here's the full algorithm `rustdoc` "
"uses to preprocess examples:"
msgstr ""

#: src/write-documentation/documentation-tests.md:62
msgid ""
"Some common `allow` attributes are inserted, including `unused_variables`, "
"`unused_assignments`, `unused_mut`, `unused_attributes`, and `dead_code`. "
"Small examples often trigger these lints."
msgstr ""

#: src/write-documentation/documentation-tests.md:66
msgid "Any attributes specified with `#![doc(test(attr(...)))]` are added."
msgstr ""

#: src/write-documentation/documentation-tests.md:67
msgid "Any leading `#![foo]` attributes are left intact as crate attributes."
msgstr ""

#: src/write-documentation/documentation-tests.md:68
msgid ""
"If the example does not contain `extern crate`, and `#!"
"[doc(test(no_crate_inject))]` was not specified, then `extern crate "
"<mycrate>;` is inserted (note the lack of `#[macro_use]`)."
msgstr ""

#: src/write-documentation/documentation-tests.md:71
msgid ""
"Finally, if the example does not contain `fn main`, the remainder of the "
"text is wrapped in `fn main() { your_code }`."
msgstr ""

#: src/write-documentation/documentation-tests.md:74
msgid "For more about that caveat in rule 4, see \"Documenting Macros\" below."
msgstr ""

#: src/write-documentation/documentation-tests.md:76
msgid "Hiding portions of the example"
msgstr ""

#: src/write-documentation/documentation-tests.md:78
msgid ""
"Sometimes, you need some setup code, or other things that would distract "
"from your example, but are important to make the tests work. Consider an "
"example block that looks like this:"
msgstr ""

#: src/write-documentation/documentation-tests.md:83
msgid ""
"/// ```\n"
"/// /// Some documentation.\n"
"/// # fn foo() {} // this function will be hidden\n"
"/// println!(\"Hello, World!\");\n"
"/// ```\n"
msgstr ""

#: src/write-documentation/documentation-tests.md:91
msgid "It will render like this:"
msgstr ""

#: src/write-documentation/documentation-tests.md:94
msgid "/// Some documentation.\n"
msgstr ""

#: src/write-documentation/documentation-tests.md:96
msgid "\"Hello, World!\""
msgstr ""

#: src/write-documentation/documentation-tests.md:99
msgid ""
"Yes, that's right: you can add lines that start with `# `, and they will be "
"hidden from the output, but will be used when compiling your code. You can "
"use this to your advantage. In this case, documentation comments need to "
"apply to some kind of function, so if I want to show you just a "
"documentation comment, I need to add a little function definition below it. "
"At the same time, it's only there to satisfy the compiler, so hiding it "
"makes the example more clear. You can use this technique to explain longer "
"examples in detail, while still preserving the testability of your "
"documentation."
msgstr ""

#: src/write-documentation/documentation-tests.md:109
msgid "For example, imagine that we wanted to document this code:"
msgstr ""

#: src/write-documentation/documentation-tests.md:114
#: src/write-documentation/documentation-tests.md:124
#: src/write-documentation/documentation-tests.md:132
#: src/write-documentation/documentation-tests.md:140
msgid "\"{}\""
msgstr ""

#: src/write-documentation/documentation-tests.md:117
msgid "We might want the documentation to end up looking like this:"
msgstr ""

#: src/write-documentation/documentation-tests.md:119
msgid "First, we set `x` to five:"
msgstr ""

#: src/write-documentation/documentation-tests.md:127
msgid "Next, we set `y` to six:"
msgstr ""

#: src/write-documentation/documentation-tests.md:135
msgid "Finally, we print the sum of `x` and `y`:"
msgstr ""

#: src/write-documentation/documentation-tests.md:143
msgid ""
"To keep each code block testable, we want the whole program in each block, "
"but we don't want the reader to see every line every time.  Here's what we "
"put in our source code:"
msgstr ""

#: src/write-documentation/documentation-tests.md:173
msgid ""
"By repeating all parts of the example, you can ensure that your example "
"still compiles, while only showing the parts that are relevant to that part "
"of your explanation."
msgstr ""

#: src/write-documentation/documentation-tests.md:177
msgid ""
"The `#`\\-hiding of lines can be prevented by using two consecutive hashes "
"`##`. This only needs to be done with the first `#` which would've otherwise "
"caused hiding. If we have a string literal like the following, which has a "
"line that starts with a `#`:"
msgstr ""

#: src/write-documentation/documentation-tests.md:183
msgid ""
"\"foo\n"
"## bar # baz\""
msgstr ""

#: src/write-documentation/documentation-tests.md:187
msgid "We can document it by escaping the initial `#`:"
msgstr ""

#: src/write-documentation/documentation-tests.md:189
msgid ""
"```text\n"
"/// let s = \"foo\n"
"/// ## bar # baz\";\n"
"```"
msgstr ""

#: src/write-documentation/documentation-tests.md:194
msgid ""
"Here is an example with a macro rule which matches on tokens starting with "
"`#`:"
msgstr ""

#: src/write-documentation/documentation-tests.md:197
msgid ""
"/// ```\n"
"/// macro_rules! ignore { (##tag) => {}; }\n"
"/// ignore! {\n"
"///     ###tag\n"
"/// }\n"
"/// ```\n"
msgstr ""

#: src/write-documentation/documentation-tests.md:206
msgid ""
"As you can see, the rule is expecting two `#`, so when calling it, we need "
"to add an extra `#` because the first one is used as escape."
msgstr ""

#: src/write-documentation/documentation-tests.md:209
msgid "Using `?` in doc tests"
msgstr ""

#: src/write-documentation/documentation-tests.md:211
msgid ""
"When writing an example, it is rarely useful to include a complete error "
"handling, as it would add significant amounts of boilerplate code. Instead, "
"you may want the following:"
msgstr ""

#: src/write-documentation/documentation-tests.md:216
msgid ""
"/// ```\n"
"/// use std::io;\n"
"/// let mut input = String::new();\n"
"/// io::stdin().read_line(&mut input)?;\n"
"/// ```\n"
msgstr ""

#: src/write-documentation/documentation-tests.md:224
msgid ""
"The problem is that `?` returns a `Result<T, E>` and test functions don't "
"return anything, so this will give a mismatched types error."
msgstr ""

#: src/write-documentation/documentation-tests.md:227
msgid ""
"You can get around this limitation by manually adding a `main` that returns "
"`Result<T, E>`, because `Result<T, E>` implements the `Termination` trait:"
msgstr ""

#: src/write-documentation/documentation-tests.md:231
msgid ""
"/// A doc test using ?\n"
"///\n"
"/// ```\n"
"/// use std::io;\n"
"///\n"
"/// fn main() -> io::Result<()> {\n"
"///     let mut input = String::new();\n"
"///     io::stdin().read_line(&mut input)?;\n"
"///     Ok(())\n"
"/// }\n"
"/// ```\n"
msgstr ""

#: src/write-documentation/documentation-tests.md:245
msgid ""
"Together with the `# ` from the section above, you arrive at a solution that "
"appears to the reader as the initial idea but works with doc tests:"
msgstr ""

#: src/write-documentation/documentation-tests.md:249
msgid ""
"/// ```\n"
"/// use std::io;\n"
"/// # fn main() -> io::Result<()> {\n"
"/// let mut input = String::new();\n"
"/// io::stdin().read_line(&mut input)?;\n"
"/// # Ok(())\n"
"/// # }\n"
"/// ```\n"
msgstr ""

#: src/write-documentation/documentation-tests.md:260
msgid ""
"As of version 1.34.0, one can also omit the `fn main()`, but you will have "
"to disambiguate the error type:"
msgstr ""

#: src/write-documentation/documentation-tests.md:264
msgid ""
"/// ```\n"
"/// use std::io;\n"
"/// let mut input = String::new();\n"
"/// io::stdin().read_line(&mut input)?;\n"
"/// # Ok::<(), io::Error>(())\n"
"/// ```\n"
msgstr ""

#: src/write-documentation/documentation-tests.md:273
msgid ""
"This is an unfortunate consequence of the `?` operator adding an implicit "
"conversion, so type inference fails because the type is not unique. Please "
"note that you must write the `(())` in one sequence without intermediate "
"whitespace so that `rustdoc` understands you want an implicit `Result`\\-"
"returning function."
msgstr ""

#: src/write-documentation/documentation-tests.md:278
msgid "Showing warnings in doctests"
msgstr ""

#: src/write-documentation/documentation-tests.md:280
msgid ""
"You can show warnings in doctests by running `rustdoc --test --test-args=--"
"show-output` (or, if you're using cargo, `cargo test --doc -- --show-"
"output`). By default, this will still hide `unused` warnings, since so many "
"examples use private functions; you can add `#![warn(unused)]` to the top of "
"your example if you want to see unused variables or dead code warnings. You "
"can also use [`#![doc(test(attr(warn(unused))))]`](the-doc-attribute."
"md#testattr) in the crate root to enable warnings globally."
msgstr ""

#: src/write-documentation/documentation-tests.md:288
msgid "Documenting macros"
msgstr ""

#: src/write-documentation/documentation-tests.md:290
msgid "Here’s an example of documenting a macro:"
msgstr ""

#: src/write-documentation/documentation-tests.md:293
msgid ""
"/// Panic with a given message unless an expression evaluates to true.\n"
"///\n"
"/// # Examples\n"
"///\n"
"/// ```\n"
"/// # #[macro_use] extern crate foo;\n"
"/// # fn main() {\n"
"/// panic_unless!(1 + 1 == 2, “Math is broken.”);\n"
"/// # }\n"
"/// ```\n"
"///\n"
"/// ```should_panic\n"
"/// # #[macro_use] extern crate foo;\n"
"/// # fn main() {\n"
"/// panic_unless!(true == false, “I’m broken.”);\n"
"/// # }\n"
"/// ```\n"
msgstr ""

#: src/write-documentation/documentation-tests.md:317
msgid ""
"You’ll note three things: we need to add our own `extern crate` line, so "
"that we can add the `#[macro_use]` attribute. Second, we’ll need to add our "
"own `main()` as well (for reasons discussed above). Finally, a judicious use "
"of `#` to comment out those two things, so they don’t show up in the output."
msgstr ""

#: src/write-documentation/documentation-tests.md:322
msgid "Attributes"
msgstr ""

#: src/write-documentation/documentation-tests.md:324
msgid ""
"Code blocks can be annotated with attributes that help `rustdoc` do the "
"right thing when testing your code:"
msgstr ""

#: src/write-documentation/documentation-tests.md:327
msgid ""
"The `ignore` attribute tells Rust to ignore your code. This is almost never "
"what you want as it's the most generic. Instead, consider annotating it with "
"`text` if it's not code or using `#`s to get a working example that only "
"shows the part you care about."
msgstr ""

#: src/write-documentation/documentation-tests.md:333
msgid ""
"/// ```ignore\n"
"/// fn foo() {\n"
"/// ```\n"
msgstr ""

#: src/write-documentation/documentation-tests.md:339
msgid ""
"`should_panic` tells `rustdoc` that the code should compile correctly but "
"panic during execution. If the code doesn't panic, the test will fail."
msgstr ""

#: src/write-documentation/documentation-tests.md:343
msgid ""
"/// ```should_panic\n"
"/// assert!(false);\n"
"/// ```\n"
msgstr ""

#: src/write-documentation/documentation-tests.md:349
msgid ""
"The `no_run` attribute will compile your code but not run it. This is "
"important for examples such as \"Here's how to retrieve a web page,\" which "
"you would want to ensure compiles, but might be run in a test environment "
"that has no network access. This attribute can also be used to demonstrate "
"code snippets that can cause Undefined Behavior."
msgstr ""

#: src/write-documentation/documentation-tests.md:356
msgid ""
"/// ```no_run\n"
"/// loop {\n"
"///     println!(\"Hello, world\");\n"
"/// }\n"
"/// ```\n"
msgstr ""

#: src/write-documentation/documentation-tests.md:364
msgid ""
"`compile_fail` tells `rustdoc` that the compilation should fail. If it "
"compiles, then the test will fail. However, please note that code failing "
"with the current Rust release may work in a future release, as new features "
"are added."
msgstr ""

#: src/write-documentation/documentation-tests.md:370
msgid ""
"/// ```compile_fail\n"
"/// let x = 5;\n"
"/// x += 2; // shouldn't compile!\n"
"/// ```\n"
msgstr ""

#: src/write-documentation/documentation-tests.md:377
msgid ""
"`edition2015`, `edition2018`, `edition2021`, and `edition2024` tell "
"`rustdoc` that the code sample should be compiled using the respective "
"edition of Rust."
msgstr ""

#: src/write-documentation/documentation-tests.md:381
msgid ""
"/// Only runs on the 2018 edition.\n"
"///\n"
"/// ```edition2018\n"
"/// let result: Result<i32, ParseIntError> = try {\n"
"///     \"1\".parse::<i32>()?\n"
"///         + \"2\".parse::<i32>()?\n"
"///         + \"3\".parse::<i32>()?\n"
"/// };\n"
"/// ```\n"
msgstr ""

#: src/write-documentation/documentation-tests.md:393
msgid ""
"Starting in the 2024 edition[^edition-note], compatible doctests are merged "
"as one before being run. We combine doctests for performance reasons: the "
"slowest part of doctests is to compile them. Merging all of them into one "
"file and compiling this new file, then running the doctests is much faster. "
"Whether doctests are merged or not, they are run in their own process."
msgstr ""

#: src/write-documentation/documentation-tests.md:398
msgid "An example of time spent when running doctests:"
msgstr ""

#: src/write-documentation/documentation-tests.md:400
msgid "[sysinfo crate](https://crates.io/crates/sysinfo):"
msgstr ""

#: src/write-documentation/documentation-tests.md:408
msgid "Rust core library:"
msgstr ""

#: src/write-documentation/documentation-tests.md:416
msgid ""
"This is based on the edition of the whole crate, not the edition of the "
"individual test case that may be specified in its code attribute."
msgstr ""

#: src/write-documentation/documentation-tests.md:419
msgid "In some cases, doctests cannot be merged. For example, if you have:"
msgstr ""

#: src/write-documentation/documentation-tests.md:422
msgid ""
"//! ```\n"
"//! let location = std::panic::Location::caller();\n"
"//! assert_eq!(location.line(), 4);\n"
"//! ```\n"
msgstr ""

#: src/write-documentation/documentation-tests.md:428
msgid ""
"The problem with this code is that, if you change any other doctests, it'll "
"likely break when running `rustdoc --test`, making it tricky to maintain."
msgstr ""

#: src/write-documentation/documentation-tests.md:431
msgid ""
"This is where the `standalone_crate` attribute comes in: it tells `rustdoc` "
"that a doctest should not be merged with the others. So the previous code "
"should use it:"
msgstr ""

#: src/write-documentation/documentation-tests.md:435
msgid ""
"//! ```standalone_crate\n"
"//! let location = std::panic::Location::caller();\n"
"//! assert_eq!(location.line(), 4);\n"
"//! ```\n"
msgstr ""

#: src/write-documentation/documentation-tests.md:441
msgid ""
"In this case, it means that the line information will not change if you add/"
"remove other doctests."
msgstr ""

#: src/write-documentation/documentation-tests.md:444
msgid "Ignoring targets"
msgstr ""

#: src/write-documentation/documentation-tests.md:446
msgid ""
"Attributes starting with `ignore-` can be used to ignore doctests for "
"specific targets. For example, `ignore-x86_64` will avoid building doctests "
"when the target name contains `x86_64`."
msgstr ""

#: src/write-documentation/documentation-tests.md:451
msgid ""
"/// ```ignore-x86_64\n"
"/// assert!(2 == 2);\n"
"/// ```\n"
msgstr ""

#: src/write-documentation/documentation-tests.md:457
msgid ""
"This doctest will not be built for targets such as `x86_64-unknown-linux-"
"gnu`."
msgstr ""

#: src/write-documentation/documentation-tests.md:459
msgid "Multiple ignore attributes can be specified to ignore multiple targets:"
msgstr ""

#: src/write-documentation/documentation-tests.md:462
msgid ""
"/// ```ignore-x86_64,ignore-windows\n"
"/// assert!(2 == 2);\n"
"/// ```\n"
msgstr ""

#: src/write-documentation/documentation-tests.md:468
msgid ""
"If you want to preserve backwards compatibility for older versions of "
"rustdoc, you can specify both `ignore` and `ignore-`, such as:"
msgstr ""

#: src/write-documentation/documentation-tests.md:472
msgid ""
"/// ```ignore,ignore-x86_64\n"
"/// assert!(2 == 2);\n"
"/// ```\n"
msgstr ""

#: src/write-documentation/documentation-tests.md:478
msgid ""
"In older versions, this will be ignored on all targets, but starting with "
"version 1.88.0, `ignore-x86_64` will override `ignore`."
msgstr ""

#: src/write-documentation/documentation-tests.md:481
msgid "Custom CSS classes for code blocks"
msgstr ""

#: src/write-documentation/documentation-tests.md:484
msgid ""
"/// ```custom,{class=language-c}\n"
"/// int main(void) { return 0; }\n"
"/// ```\n"
msgstr ""

#: src/write-documentation/documentation-tests.md:490
msgid ""
"The text `int main(void) { return 0; }` is rendered without highlighting in "
"a code block with the class `language-c`. This can be used to highlight "
"other languages through JavaScript libraries for example."
msgstr ""

#: src/write-documentation/documentation-tests.md:494
msgid ""
"Without the `custom` attribute, it would be generated as a Rust code example "
"with an additional `language-C` CSS class. Therefore, if you specifically "
"don't want it to be a Rust code example, don't forget to add the `custom` "
"attribute."
msgstr ""

#: src/write-documentation/documentation-tests.md:498
msgid ""
"To be noted that you can replace `class=` with `.` to achieve the same "
"result:"
msgstr ""

#: src/write-documentation/documentation-tests.md:501
msgid ""
"/// ```custom,{.language-c}\n"
"/// int main(void) { return 0; }\n"
"/// ```\n"
msgstr ""

#: src/write-documentation/documentation-tests.md:507
msgid ""
"To be noted, `rust` and `.rust`/`class=rust` have different effects: `rust` "
"indicates that this is a Rust code block whereas the two others add a "
"\"rust\" CSS class on the code block."
msgstr ""

#: src/write-documentation/documentation-tests.md:510
msgid "You can also use double quotes:"
msgstr ""

#: src/write-documentation/documentation-tests.md:513
msgid ""
"/// ```\"not rust\" {.\"hello everyone\"}\n"
"/// int main(void) { return 0; }\n"
"/// ```\n"
msgstr ""

#: src/write-documentation/documentation-tests.md:519
msgid "Syntax reference"
msgstr ""

#: src/write-documentation/documentation-tests.md:521
msgid ""
"The _exact_ syntax for code blocks, including the edge cases, can be found "
"in the [Fenced Code Blocks](https://spec.commonmark.org/0.29/#fenced-code-"
"blocks) section of the CommonMark specification."
msgstr ""

#: src/write-documentation/documentation-tests.md:525
msgid ""
"Rustdoc also accepts _indented_ code blocks as an alternative to fenced code "
"blocks: instead of surrounding your code with three backticks, you can "
"indent each line by four or more spaces."
msgstr ""

#: src/write-documentation/documentation-tests.md:534
msgid ""
"These, too, are documented in the CommonMark specification, in the [Indented "
"Code Blocks](https://spec.commonmark.org/0.29/#indented-code-blocks) section."
msgstr ""

#: src/write-documentation/documentation-tests.md:538
msgid ""
"However, it's preferable to use fenced code blocks over indented code "
"blocks. Not only are fenced code blocks considered more idiomatic for Rust "
"code, but there is no way to use attributes such as `ignore` or "
"`should_panic` with indented code blocks."
msgstr ""

#: src/write-documentation/documentation-tests.md:543
msgid "Include items only when collecting doctests"
msgstr ""

#: src/write-documentation/documentation-tests.md:545
msgid ""
"Rustdoc's documentation tests can do some things that regular unit tests "
"can't, so it can sometimes be useful to extend your doctests with samples "
"that wouldn't otherwise need to be in documentation. To this end, Rustdoc "
"allows you to have certain items only appear when it's collecting doctests, "
"so you can utilize doctest functionality without forcing the test to appear "
"in docs, or to find an arbitrary private item to include it on."
msgstr ""

#: src/write-documentation/documentation-tests.md:551
msgid ""
"When compiling a crate for use in doctests (with `--test` option), `rustdoc` "
"will set `#[cfg(doctest)]`. Note that they will still link against only the "
"public items of your crate; if you need to test private items, you need to "
"write a unit test."
msgstr ""

#: src/write-documentation/documentation-tests.md:555
msgid ""
"In this example, we're adding doctests that we know won't compile, to verify "
"that our struct can only take in valid data:"
msgstr ""

#: src/write-documentation/documentation-tests.md:559
msgid ""
"/// We have a struct here. Remember it doesn't accept negative numbers!\n"
msgstr ""

#: src/write-documentation/documentation-tests.md:561
msgid ""
"/// ```compile_fail\n"
"/// let x = my_crate::MyStruct(-5);\n"
"/// ```\n"
msgstr ""

#: src/write-documentation/documentation-tests.md:569
msgid ""
"Note that the struct `MyStructOnlyTakesUsize` here isn't actually part of "
"your public crate API. The use of `#[cfg(doctest)]` makes sure that this "
"struct only exists while `rustdoc` is collecting doctests. This means that "
"its doctest is executed when `--test` is passed to rustdoc, but is hidden "
"from the public documentation."
msgstr ""

#: src/write-documentation/documentation-tests.md:574
msgid ""
"Another possible use of `#[cfg(doctest)]` is to test doctests that are "
"included in your README file without including it in your main "
"documentation. For example, you could write this into your `lib.rs` to test "
"your README as part of your doctests:"
msgstr ""

#: src/write-documentation/documentation-tests.md:579
msgid "\"../README.md\""
msgstr ""

#: src/write-documentation/documentation-tests.md:584
msgid ""
"This will include your README as documentation on the hidden struct "
"`ReadmeDoctests`, which will then be tested alongside the rest of your "
"doctests."
msgstr ""

#: src/write-documentation/documentation-tests.md:587
msgid "Controlling the compilation and run directories"
msgstr ""

#: src/write-documentation/documentation-tests.md:589
msgid ""
"By default, `rustdoc --test` will compile and run documentation test "
"examples from the same working directory. The compilation directory is being "
"used for compiler diagnostics, the `file!()` macro and the output of "
"`rustdoc` test runner itself, whereas the run directory has an influence on "
"file-system operations within documentation test examples, such as `std::fs::"
"read_to_string`."
msgstr ""

#: src/write-documentation/documentation-tests.md:595
msgid ""
"The `--test-run-directory` flag allows controlling the run directory "
"separately from the compilation directory. This is particularly useful in "
"workspaces, where compiler invocations and thus diagnostics should be "
"relative to the workspace directory, but documentation test examples should "
"run relative to the crate directory."
msgstr ""

#: src/write-documentation/linking-to-items-by-name.md:1
msgid "Linking to items by name"
msgstr ""

#: src/write-documentation/linking-to-items-by-name.md:3
msgid ""
"Rustdoc is capable of directly linking to other rustdoc pages using the path "
"of the item as a link. This is referred to as an 'intra-doc link'."
msgstr ""

#: src/write-documentation/linking-to-items-by-name.md:6
msgid ""
"For example, in the following code all of the links will link to the rustdoc "
"page for `Bar`:"
msgstr ""

#: src/write-documentation/linking-to-items-by-name.md:9
msgid "/// This struct is not [Bar]\n"
msgstr ""

#: src/write-documentation/linking-to-items-by-name.md:11
msgid "/// This struct is also not [bar](Bar)\n"
msgstr ""

#: src/write-documentation/linking-to-items-by-name.md:14
msgid ""
"/// This struct is also not [bar][b]\n"
"///\n"
"/// [b]: Bar\n"
msgstr ""

#: src/write-documentation/linking-to-items-by-name.md:19
msgid "/// This struct is also not [`Bar`]\n"
msgstr ""

#: src/write-documentation/linking-to-items-by-name.md:22
msgid "/// This struct *is* [`Bar`]!\n"
msgstr ""

#: src/write-documentation/linking-to-items-by-name.md:27
msgid ""
"Unlike normal Markdown, `[bar][Bar]` syntax is also supported without "
"needing a `[Bar]: ...` reference link."
msgstr ""

#: src/write-documentation/linking-to-items-by-name.md:30
msgid ""
"Backticks around the link will be stripped, so ``[`Option`]`` will correctly "
"link to `Option`."
msgstr ""

#: src/write-documentation/linking-to-items-by-name.md:33
msgid "Valid links"
msgstr ""

#: src/write-documentation/linking-to-items-by-name.md:35
msgid ""
"You can refer to anything in scope, and use paths, including `Self`, `self`, "
"`super`, and `crate`. Associated items (functions, types, and constants) are "
"supported, but [not for blanket trait implementations](https://github.com/"
"rust-lang/rust/pull/79682). Rustdoc also supports linking to all primitives "
"listed in [the standard library documentation](../../std/index."
"html#primitives)."
msgstr ""

#: src/write-documentation/linking-to-items-by-name.md:42
msgid ""
"You can also refer to items with generic parameters like `Vec<T>`. The link "
"will resolve as if you had written ``[`Vec<T>`](Vec)``. Fully-qualified "
"syntax (for example, `<Vec as IntoIterator>::into_iter()`) is [not yet "
"supported](https://github.com/rust-lang/rust/issues/74563), however."
msgstr ""

#: src/write-documentation/linking-to-items-by-name.md:50
msgid ""
"/// This is a version of [`Receiver<T>`] with support for [`std::future`].\n"
"///\n"
"/// You can obtain a [`std::future::Future`] by calling [`Self::recv()`].\n"
msgstr ""

#: src/write-documentation/linking-to-items-by-name.md:65
msgid "Rustdoc allows using URL fragment specifiers, just like a normal link:"
msgstr ""

#: src/write-documentation/linking-to-items-by-name.md:68
msgid ""
"/// This is a special implementation of [positional parameters].\n"
"///\n"
"/// [positional parameters]: std::fmt#formatting-parameters\n"
msgstr ""

#: src/write-documentation/linking-to-items-by-name.md:74
msgid "Namespaces and Disambiguators"
msgstr ""

#: src/write-documentation/linking-to-items-by-name.md:76
msgid ""
"Paths in Rust have three namespaces: type, value, and macro. Item names must "
"be unique within their namespace, but can overlap with items in other "
"namespaces. In case of ambiguity, rustdoc will warn about the ambiguity and "
"suggest a disambiguator."
msgstr ""

#: src/write-documentation/linking-to-items-by-name.md:81
msgid "/// See also: [`Foo`](struct@Foo)\n"
msgstr ""

#: src/write-documentation/linking-to-items-by-name.md:83
msgid "/// This is different from [`Foo`](fn@Foo)\n"
msgstr ""

#: src/write-documentation/linking-to-items-by-name.md:90
msgid ""
"These prefixes will be stripped when displayed in the documentation, so "
"`[struct@Foo]` will be rendered as `Foo`. The following prefixes are "
"available: `struct`, `enum`, `trait`, `union`, `mod`, `module`, `const`, "
"`constant`, `fn`, `function`, `field`, `variant`, `method`, `derive`, "
"`type`, `value`, `macro`, `tyalias`, `typealias`, `prim` or `primitive`."
msgstr ""

#: src/write-documentation/linking-to-items-by-name.md:95
msgid ""
"You can also disambiguate for functions by adding `()` after the function "
"name, or for macros by adding `!` after the macro name. The macro `!` can be "
"followed by `()`, `{}`, or `[]`. Example:"
msgstr ""

#: src/write-documentation/linking-to-items-by-name.md:100
msgid "/// This is different from [`foo!()`].\n"
msgstr ""

#: src/write-documentation/linking-to-items-by-name.md:102
msgid "/// This is different from [`foo()`]\n"
msgstr ""

#: src/write-documentation/linking-to-items-by-name.md:109
msgid ""
"There is one case where the disambiguation will be performed automatically: "
"if an intra doc link is resolved at the same time as a trait and as a derive "
"proc-macro. In this case, it'll always generate a link to the trait and not "
"emit a \"missing disambiguation\" warning. A good example of this case is "
"when you link to the `Clone` trait: there is also a `Clone` proc-macro but "
"it ignores it in this case. If you want to link to the proc-macro, you can "
"use the `macro@` disambiguator."
msgstr ""

#: src/write-documentation/linking-to-items-by-name.md:116
msgid "Warnings, re-exports, and scoping"
msgstr ""

#: src/write-documentation/linking-to-items-by-name.md:118
msgid ""
"Links are resolved in the scope of the module where the item is defined, "
"even when the item is re-exported. If a link from another crate fails to "
"resolve, no warning is given."
msgstr ""

#: src/write-documentation/linking-to-items-by-name.md:124
msgid "/// Link to [f()]\n"
msgstr ""

#: src/write-documentation/linking-to-items-by-name.md:128
msgid "// the link to `f` will still resolve correctly\n"
msgstr ""

#: src/write-documentation/linking-to-items-by-name.md:131
msgid ""
"When re-exporting an item, rustdoc allows adding additional documentation to "
"it. That additional documentation will be resolved in the scope of the re-"
"export, not the original, allowing you to link to items in the new crate. "
"The new links will still give a warning if they fail to resolve."
msgstr ""

#: src/write-documentation/linking-to-items-by-name.md:137
msgid "/// See also [foo()]\n"
msgstr ""

#: src/write-documentation/linking-to-items-by-name.md:143
msgid ""
"This is especially useful for proc-macros, which must always be defined in "
"their own dedicated crate."
msgstr ""

#: src/write-documentation/linking-to-items-by-name.md:145
msgid ""
"Note: Because of how `macro_rules!` macros are scoped in Rust, the intra-doc "
"links of a `macro_rules!` macro will be resolved [relative to the crate root]"
"(https://github.com/rust-lang/rust/issues/72243), as opposed to the module "
"it is defined in."
msgstr ""

#: src/write-documentation/linking-to-items-by-name.md:149
msgid ""
"If links do not look 'sufficiently like' an intra-doc link, they will be "
"ignored and no warning will be given, even if the link fails to resolve. For "
"example, any link containing `/` or `[]` characters will be ignored."
msgstr ""

#: src/write-documentation/linking-to-items-by-name.md:155
msgid "What happens in case an intra-doc link cannot be generated"
msgstr ""

#: src/write-documentation/linking-to-items-by-name.md:157
msgid ""
"In some cases (items behind a `cfg` for example), an intra-doc link cannot "
"be generated to item. There are different ways to create a link in markdown, "
"and depending on the one you use, it will render differently in this case:"
msgstr ""

#: src/write-documentation/linking-to-items-by-name.md:170
msgid ""
"`1.` and `2.` will be displayed as is in the rendered documentation (ie, "
"`[a]` and `[b][c]`) whereas `3.` and `4.` will be replaced by a link "
"targeting `e` for `[d](e)` and `g` for `[f]`."
msgstr ""

#: src/write-documentation/re-exports.md:1
msgid "Re-exports"
msgstr ""

#: src/write-documentation/re-exports.md:3
msgid ""
"Let's start by explaining what are re-exports. To do so, we will use an "
"example where we are writing a library (named `lib`) with some types "
"dispatched in sub-modules:"
msgstr ""

#: src/write-documentation/re-exports.md:15
msgid "Users can import them like this:"
msgstr ""

#: src/write-documentation/re-exports.md:22
msgid ""
"But what if you want the types to be available directly at the crate root or "
"if we don't want the modules to be visible for users? That's where re-"
"exports come in:"
msgstr ""

#: src/write-documentation/re-exports.md:26
msgid "// `sub_module1` and `sub_module2` are not visible outside.\n"
msgstr ""

#: src/write-documentation/re-exports.md:32
msgid "// We re-export both types:\n"
msgstr ""

#: src/write-documentation/re-exports.md:38
msgid "And now users will be able to do:"
msgstr ""

#: src/write-documentation/re-exports.md:44
msgid ""
"And since both `sub_module1` and `sub_module2` are private, users won't be "
"able to import them."
msgstr ""

#: src/write-documentation/re-exports.md:46
msgid ""
"Now what's interesting is that the generated documentation for this crate "
"will show both `Foo` and `AnotherFoo` directly at the crate root, meaning "
"they have been inlined. There are a few rules to know whether or not a re-"
"exported item will be inlined."
msgstr ""

#: src/write-documentation/re-exports.md:50
msgid "Inlining rules"
msgstr ""

#: src/write-documentation/re-exports.md:52
msgid "If a public item comes from a private module, it will be inlined:"
msgstr ""

#: src/write-documentation/re-exports.md:59
msgid "// `Public` will inlined here since `private_module` is private.\n"
msgstr ""

#: src/write-documentation/re-exports.md:61
msgid "// `Public` will not be inlined here since `public_mod` is public.\n"
msgstr ""

#: src/write-documentation/re-exports.md:66
msgid ""
"Likewise, if an item inherits `#[doc(hidden)]` from any of its ancestors, it "
"will be inlined:"
msgstr ""

#: src/write-documentation/re-exports.md:72
msgid ""
"// `Public` be inlined since its parent (`public_mod`) has "
"`#[doc(hidden)]`.\n"
msgstr ""

#: src/write-documentation/re-exports.md:77
msgid ""
"If an item has `#[doc(hidden)]`, it won't be inlined (nor visible in the "
"generated documentation):"
msgstr ""

#: src/write-documentation/re-exports.md:80
#: src/write-documentation/re-exports.md:92
msgid "// This struct won't be visible.\n"
msgstr ""

#: src/write-documentation/re-exports.md:83
msgid "// This re-export won't be visible.\n"
msgstr ""

#: src/write-documentation/re-exports.md:88
msgid ""
"However, if you still want the re-export itself to be visible, you can add "
"the `#[doc(inline)]` attribute on it:"
msgstr ""

#: src/write-documentation/re-exports.md:100
msgid ""
"In this case, you will have `pub use self::Hidden as InlinedHidden;` in the "
"generated documentation but no link to the `Hidden` item."
msgstr ""

#: src/write-documentation/re-exports.md:103
msgid ""
"So back to `#[doc(hidden)]`: if you have multiple re-exports and some of "
"them have `#[doc(hidden)]`, then these ones (and only these) won't appear in "
"the documentation:"
msgstr ""

#: src/write-documentation/re-exports.md:108
#: src/write-documentation/re-exports.md:160
#: src/write-documentation/re-exports.md:193
#: src/write-documentation/re-exports.md:207
msgid "/// First\n"
msgstr ""

#: src/write-documentation/re-exports.md:111
#: src/write-documentation/re-exports.md:163
msgid "/// Second\n"
msgstr ""

#: src/write-documentation/re-exports.md:114
#: src/write-documentation/re-exports.md:167
msgid "/// Third\n"
msgstr ""

#: src/write-documentation/re-exports.md:119
msgid ""
"In this case, `InPrivate` will be inlined as `Visible`. However, its "
"documentation will be `First Third` and not `First Second Third` because the "
"re-export with `Second` as documentation has `#[doc(hidden)]`, therefore, "
"all its attributes are ignored."
msgstr ""

#: src/write-documentation/re-exports.md:123
msgid "Inlining with `#[doc(inline)]`"
msgstr ""

#: src/write-documentation/re-exports.md:125
msgid ""
"You can use the `#[doc(inline)]` attribute if you want to force an item to "
"be inlined:"
msgstr ""

#: src/write-documentation/re-exports.md:135
msgid ""
"With this code, even though `public_mod::Public` is public and present in "
"the documentation, the `Public` type will be present both at the crate root "
"and in the `public_mod` module."
msgstr ""

#: src/write-documentation/re-exports.md:138
msgid "Preventing inlining with `#[doc(no_inline)]`"
msgstr ""

#: src/write-documentation/re-exports.md:140
msgid ""
"On the opposite of the `#[doc(inline)]` attribute, if you want to prevent an "
"item from being inlined, you can use `#[doc(no_inline)]`:"
msgstr ""

#: src/write-documentation/re-exports.md:151
msgid ""
"In the generated documentation, you will see a re-export at the crate root "
"and not the type directly."
msgstr ""

#: src/write-documentation/re-exports.md:156
msgid ""
"When an item is inlined, its doc comments and most of its attributes will be "
"inlined along with it:"
msgstr ""

#: src/write-documentation/re-exports.md:174
msgid ""
"In this case, `Visible` will have as documentation `First Second Third` and "
"will also have as `cfg`: `#[cfg(a, b, c)]`."
msgstr ""

#: src/write-documentation/re-exports.md:177
msgid ""
"[Intra-doc links](./linking-to-items-by-name.md) are resolved relative to "
"where the doc comment is defined."
msgstr ""

#: src/write-documentation/re-exports.md:180
msgid "There are a few attributes which are not inlined though:"
msgstr ""

#: src/write-documentation/re-exports.md:181
msgid "`#[doc(alias=\"\")]`"
msgstr ""

#: src/write-documentation/re-exports.md:182
msgid "`#[doc(inline)]`"
msgstr ""

#: src/write-documentation/re-exports.md:183
msgid "`#[doc(no_inline)]`"
msgstr ""

#: src/write-documentation/re-exports.md:184
msgid ""
"`#[doc(hidden)]` (because the re-export itself and its attributes are "
"ignored)."
msgstr ""

#: src/write-documentation/re-exports.md:186
msgid ""
"All other attributes are inherited when inlined, so that the documentation "
"matches the behavior if the inlined item was directly defined at the spot "
"where it's shown."
msgstr ""

#: src/write-documentation/re-exports.md:189
msgid "These rules also apply if the item is inlined with a glob re-export:"
msgstr ""

#: src/write-documentation/re-exports.md:202
msgid ""
"Otherwise, the attributes displayed will be from the re-exported item and "
"the attributes on the re-export itself will be ignored:"
msgstr ""

#: src/write-documentation/re-exports.md:216
msgid "In the above case, `cfg(c)` will not be displayed in the docs."
msgstr ""

#: src/write-documentation/the-doc-attribute.md:1
msgid "The `#[doc]` attribute"
msgstr ""

#: src/write-documentation/the-doc-attribute.md:3
msgid ""
"The `#[doc]` attribute lets you control various aspects of how `rustdoc` "
"does its job."
msgstr ""

#: src/write-documentation/the-doc-attribute.md:6
msgid ""
"The most basic function of `#[doc]` is to handle the actual documentation "
"text. That is, `///` is syntax sugar for `#[doc]` (as is `//!` for `#!"
"[doc]`). This means that these two are the same:"
msgstr ""

#: src/write-documentation/the-doc-attribute.md:11
msgid "/// This is a doc comment.\n"
msgstr ""

#: src/write-documentation/the-doc-attribute.md:12
msgid "\" This is a doc comment.\""
msgstr ""

#: src/write-documentation/the-doc-attribute.md:16
msgid ""
"(Note the leading space and the raw string literal in the attribute version.)"
msgstr ""

#: src/write-documentation/the-doc-attribute.md:18
msgid ""
"In most cases, `///` is easier to use than `#[doc]`. One case where the "
"latter is easier is when generating documentation in macros; the `collapse-"
"docs` pass will combine multiple `#[doc]` attributes into a single doc "
"comment, letting you generate code like this:"
msgstr ""

#: src/write-documentation/the-doc-attribute.md:23
msgid "\"This is\""
msgstr ""

#: src/write-documentation/the-doc-attribute.md:24
msgid "\" a \""
msgstr ""

#: src/write-documentation/the-doc-attribute.md:25
msgid "\"doc comment\""
msgstr ""

#: src/write-documentation/the-doc-attribute.md:29
msgid "Which can feel more flexible. Note that this would generate this:"
msgstr ""

#: src/write-documentation/the-doc-attribute.md:32
msgid "\"This is\\n a \\ndoc comment\""
msgstr ""

#: src/write-documentation/the-doc-attribute.md:36
msgid ""
"but given that docs are rendered via Markdown, it will remove these newlines."
msgstr ""

#: src/write-documentation/the-doc-attribute.md:38
msgid "Another use case is for including external files as documentation:"
msgstr ""

#: src/write-documentation/the-doc-attribute.md:41
msgid "\"../../README.md\""
msgstr ""

#: src/write-documentation/the-doc-attribute.md:45
msgid ""
"The `doc` attribute has more options though! These don't involve the text of "
"the output, but instead, various aspects of the presentation of the output. "
"We've split them into two kinds below: attributes that are useful at the "
"crate level, and ones that are useful at the item level."
msgstr ""

#: src/write-documentation/the-doc-attribute.md:50
msgid "At the crate level"
msgstr ""

#: src/write-documentation/the-doc-attribute.md:52
msgid "These options control how the docs look at a crate level."
msgstr ""

#: src/write-documentation/the-doc-attribute.md:54
msgid "`html_favicon_url`"
msgstr ""

#: src/write-documentation/the-doc-attribute.md:56
msgid ""
"This form of the `doc` attribute lets you control the favicon of your docs."
msgstr ""

#: src/write-documentation/the-doc-attribute.md:59
msgid "\"https://example.com/favicon.ico\""
msgstr ""

#: src/write-documentation/the-doc-attribute.md:62
msgid ""
"This will put `<link rel=\"icon\" href=\"{}\">` into your docs, where the "
"string for the attribute goes into the `{}`."
msgstr ""

#: src/write-documentation/the-doc-attribute.md:65
msgid "If you don't use this attribute, a default favicon will be used."
msgstr ""

#: src/write-documentation/the-doc-attribute.md:67
msgid "`html_logo_url`"
msgstr ""

#: src/write-documentation/the-doc-attribute.md:69
msgid ""
"This form of the `doc` attribute lets you control the logo in the upper left "
"hand side of the docs."
msgstr ""

#: src/write-documentation/the-doc-attribute.md:73
msgid "\"https://example.com/logo.jpg\""
msgstr ""

#: src/write-documentation/the-doc-attribute.md:76
msgid ""
"This will put `<a href='../index.html'><img src='{}' alt='logo' "
"width='100'></a>` into your docs, where the string for the attribute goes "
"into the `{}`."
msgstr ""

#: src/write-documentation/the-doc-attribute.md:79
msgid "If you don't use this attribute, there will be no logo."
msgstr ""

#: src/write-documentation/the-doc-attribute.md:81
msgid "`html_playground_url`"
msgstr ""

#: src/write-documentation/the-doc-attribute.md:83
msgid ""
"This form of the `doc` attribute lets you control where the \"run\" buttons "
"on your documentation examples make requests to."
msgstr ""

#: src/write-documentation/the-doc-attribute.md:87
msgid "\"https://playground.example.com/\""
msgstr ""

#: src/write-documentation/the-doc-attribute.md:90
msgid ""
"Now, when you press \"run\", the button will make a request to this domain. "
"The request URL will contain 3 query parameters:"
msgstr ""

#: src/write-documentation/the-doc-attribute.md:92
msgid "`code` for the code in the documentation"
msgstr ""

#: src/write-documentation/the-doc-attribute.md:93
msgid ""
"`version` for the Rust channel, e.g. nightly, which is decided by whether "
"`code` contain unstable features"
msgstr ""

#: src/write-documentation/the-doc-attribute.md:94
msgid "`edition` for the Rust edition, e.g. 2024"
msgstr ""

#: src/write-documentation/the-doc-attribute.md:96
msgid "If you don't use this attribute, there will be no run buttons."
msgstr ""

#: src/write-documentation/the-doc-attribute.md:98
msgid "`issue_tracker_base_url`"
msgstr ""

#: src/write-documentation/the-doc-attribute.md:100
msgid ""
"This form of the `doc` attribute is mostly only useful for the standard "
"library; When a feature is unstable, an issue number for tracking the "
"feature must be given. `rustdoc` uses this number, plus the base URL given "
"here, to link to the tracking issue."
msgstr ""

#: src/write-documentation/the-doc-attribute.md:106
msgid "\"https://github.com/rust-lang/rust/issues/\""
msgstr ""

#: src/write-documentation/the-doc-attribute.md:109
msgid "`html_root_url`"
msgstr ""

#: src/write-documentation/the-doc-attribute.md:111
msgid ""
"The `#[doc(html_root_url = \"…\")]` attribute value indicates the URL for "
"generating links to external crates. When rustdoc needs to generate a link "
"to an item in an external crate, it will first check if the extern crate has "
"been documented locally on-disk, and if so link directly to it. Failing "
"that, it will use the URL given by the `--extern-html-root-url` command-line "
"flag if available. If that is not available, then it will use the "
"`html_root_url` value in the extern crate if it is available. If that is not "
"available, then the extern items will not be linked."
msgstr ""

#: src/write-documentation/the-doc-attribute.md:121
msgid "\"https://docs.rs/serde/1.0\""
msgstr ""

#: src/write-documentation/the-doc-attribute.md:124
msgid "`html_no_source`"
msgstr ""

#: src/write-documentation/the-doc-attribute.md:126
msgid ""
"By default, `rustdoc` will include the source code of your program, with "
"links to it in the docs. But if you include this:"
msgstr ""

#: src/write-documentation/the-doc-attribute.md:133
#: src/write-documentation/the-doc-attribute.md:144
msgid "it will not."
msgstr ""

#: src/write-documentation/the-doc-attribute.md:135
msgid "`test(no_crate_inject)`"
msgstr ""

#: src/write-documentation/the-doc-attribute.md:137
msgid ""
"By default, `rustdoc` will automatically add a line with `extern crate "
"my_crate;` into each doctest. But if you include this:"
msgstr ""

#: src/write-documentation/the-doc-attribute.md:146
msgid "At the item level"
msgstr ""

#: src/write-documentation/the-doc-attribute.md:148
msgid ""
"These forms of the `#[doc]` attribute are used on individual items, to "
"control how they are documented."
msgstr ""

#: src/write-documentation/the-doc-attribute.md:151
msgid "`inline` and `no_inline`"
msgstr ""

#: src/write-documentation/the-doc-attribute.md:153
msgid "<span id=\"docno_inlinedocinline\"></span>"
msgstr ""

#: src/write-documentation/the-doc-attribute.md:155
msgid ""
"These attributes are used on `use` statements, and control where the "
"documentation shows up. For example, consider this Rust code:"
msgstr ""

#: src/write-documentation/the-doc-attribute.md:160
#: src/write-documentation/the-doc-attribute.md:188
#: src/write-documentation/the-doc-attribute.md:205
msgid "/// bar docs\n"
msgstr ""

#: src/write-documentation/the-doc-attribute.md:163
#: src/write-documentation/the-doc-attribute.md:191
#: src/write-documentation/the-doc-attribute.md:208
msgid "/// the docs for Bar\n"
msgstr ""

#: src/write-documentation/the-doc-attribute.md:169
msgid ""
"The documentation will generate a \"Re-exports\" section, and say `pub use "
"bar::Bar;`, where `Bar` is a link to its page."
msgstr ""

#: src/write-documentation/the-doc-attribute.md:172
msgid "If we change the `use` line like this:"
msgstr ""

#: src/write-documentation/the-doc-attribute.md:181
msgid ""
"Instead, `Bar` will appear in a `Structs` section, just like `Bar` was "
"defined at the top level, rather than `pub use`'d."
msgstr ""

#: src/write-documentation/the-doc-attribute.md:184
msgid "Let's change our original example, by making `bar` private:"
msgstr ""

#: src/write-documentation/the-doc-attribute.md:197
msgid ""
"Here, because `bar` is not public, `bar` wouldn't have its own page, so "
"there's nowhere to link to. `rustdoc` will inline these definitions, and so "
"we end up in the same case as the `#[doc(inline)]` above; `Bar` is in a "
"`Structs` section, as if it were defined at the top level. If we add the "
"`no_inline` form of the attribute:"
msgstr ""

#: src/write-documentation/the-doc-attribute.md:214
msgid ""
"Now we'll have a `Re-exports` line, and `Bar` will not link to anywhere."
msgstr ""

#: src/write-documentation/the-doc-attribute.md:216
msgid ""
"One special case: In Rust 2018 and later, if you `pub use` one of your "
"dependencies, `rustdoc` will not eagerly inline it as a module unless you "
"add `#[doc(inline)]`."
msgstr ""

#: src/write-documentation/the-doc-attribute.md:219
msgid ""
"If you want to know more about inlining rules, take a look at the [`re-"
"exports` chapter](./re-exports.md)."
msgstr ""

#: src/write-documentation/the-doc-attribute.md:222
msgid "`hidden`"
msgstr ""

#: src/write-documentation/the-doc-attribute.md:224
msgid "<span id=\"dochidden\"></span>"
msgstr ""

#: src/write-documentation/the-doc-attribute.md:226
msgid ""
"Any item annotated with `#[doc(hidden)]` will not appear in the "
"documentation, unless the [`--document-hidden-items`](../unstable-features."
"md#document-hidden-items) flag is used."
msgstr ""

#: src/write-documentation/the-doc-attribute.md:229
msgid ""
"You can find more information in the [`re-exports` chapter](./re-exports.md)."
msgstr ""

#: src/write-documentation/the-doc-attribute.md:231
msgid "`alias`"
msgstr ""

#: src/write-documentation/the-doc-attribute.md:233
msgid "This attribute adds an alias in the search index."
msgstr ""

#: src/write-documentation/the-doc-attribute.md:235
msgid "Let's take an example:"
msgstr ""

#: src/write-documentation/the-doc-attribute.md:238
msgid "\"TheAlias\""
msgstr ""

#: src/write-documentation/the-doc-attribute.md:242
msgid ""
"So now, if you enter \"TheAlias\" in the search, it'll display `SomeType`. "
"Of course, if you enter `SomeType` it'll return `SomeType` as expected!"
msgstr ""

#: src/write-documentation/the-doc-attribute.md:245
msgid "FFI example"
msgstr ""

#: src/write-documentation/the-doc-attribute.md:247
msgid ""
"This doc attribute is especially useful when writing bindings for a C "
"library. For example, let's say we have a C function that looks like this:"
msgstr ""

#: src/write-documentation/the-doc-attribute.md:254
msgid ""
"It takes a pointer to an `Obj` type and returns an integer. In Rust, it "
"might be written like this:"
msgstr ""

#: src/write-documentation/the-doc-attribute.md:269
msgid ""
"The function has been turned into a method to make it more convenient to "
"use. However, if you want to look for the Rust equivalent of "
"`lib_name_do_something`, you have no way to do so."
msgstr ""

#: src/write-documentation/the-doc-attribute.md:273
msgid ""
"To get around this limitation, we just add `#[doc(alias = "
"\"lib_name_do_something\")]` on the `do_something` method and then it's all "
"good! Users can now look for `lib_name_do_something` in our crate directly "
"and find `Obj::do_something`."
msgstr ""

#: src/write-documentation/the-doc-attribute.md:278
msgid "`test(attr(...))`"
msgstr ""

