#: src/what-is-rustdoc.md:108
msgid "Outer and inner documentation"
msgstr ""

#: src/what-is-rustdoc.md:110
msgid ""
"The `///` syntax is used to document the item present after it. That's why "
"it is called an outer documentation. There is another syntax: `//!`, which "
"is used to document the item it is present inside. It is called an inner "
"documentation. It is often used when documenting the entire crate, because "
"nothing comes before it: it is the root of the crate. So in order to "
"document an entire crate, you need to use `//!` syntax. For example:"
msgstr ""

#: src/what-is-rustdoc.md:120
msgid "//! This is my first rust crate\n"
msgstr ""

#: src/what-is-rustdoc.md:123
msgid ""
"When used in the crate root, it documents the item it is inside, which is "
"the crate itself."
msgstr ""

#: src/what-is-rustdoc.md:126
msgid ""
"For more information about the `//!` syntax, see [the Book](https://doc.rust-"
"lang.org/book/ch14-02-publishing-to-crates-io.html#commenting-contained-"
"items)."
msgstr ""

#: src/what-is-rustdoc.md:131
msgid "Using standalone Markdown files"
msgstr ""

#: src/what-is-rustdoc.md:133
msgid ""
"`rustdoc` can also generate HTML from standalone Markdown files. Let' s give "
"it a try: create a `README.md` file with these contents:"
msgstr ""

#: src/what-is-rustdoc.md:136
msgid ""
"````text\n"
"# Docs\n"
"\n"
"This is a project to test out `rustdoc`.\n"
"\n"
"[Here is a link!](https://www.rust-lang.org)\n"
"\n"
"## Example\n"
"\n"
"```rust\n"
"fn foo() -> i32 {\n"
"    1 + 1\n"
"}\n"
"```\n"
"````"
msgstr ""

#: src/what-is-rustdoc.md:152
msgid "And call `rustdoc` on it:"
msgstr ""

#: src/what-is-rustdoc.md:158
msgid ""
"You will find an HTML file in `docs/doc/README.html` generated from its "
"Markdown contents."
msgstr ""

#: src/what-is-rustdoc.md:161
msgid ""
"Cargo currently does not understand standalone Markdown files, unfortunately."
msgstr ""

